      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the magnitude (or absolute value) of a complex number, we apply the formula:\n\n\\[\n|z| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n\\]\n\nwhere $z = a + bi$ is a complex number, and $a$ and $b$ are its real and imaginary parts respectively.\n\nFor the given complex number $\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{8}i$, we can identify $a = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $b = -\\frac{3}{8}$. Plugging these values into the formula, we have:\n\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the squares:\n\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{9}{64}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, we need a common denominator. The common denominator of 4 and 64 is 64:\n\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{16}{64}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{9}{64}\\right)}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{64}}\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root yields:\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{\\sqrt{64}}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the magnitude (absolute value) of the complex number $\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{8}i$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{8}}\n\\]",
